## Approach

Cell (i, j) is black exactly when j ≤ r_i (row prefix) and i ≤ c_j (column prefix). So the black cells must form a Young diagram anchored at the top-left corner: if a cell is black, every cell above it and to its left must also be black.

This gives a simple feasibility test:

- **Necessary:** If a white cell (x1, y1) lies weakly up-left of a black cell (x2, y2), i.e. x1 ≤ x2 and y1 ≤ y2, the answer is No. The black cell forces (x1, y1) to be black.
- **Sufficient:** Take the set of all cells weakly up-left of some given black cell. This is a valid Young diagram, it contains every given black cell, and it contains no given white cell when the necessary condition holds. Filling the grid this way satisfies every row and column.

So the answer is **No** if and only if some white cell is weakly up-left of some black cell.

## Checking the samples

- **Sample 1:** The only white cell is (3,2). No black cell is weakly down-right of it ((4,1) fails on column, (1,3) fails on row). Output Yes, which matches.
- **Sample 2:** White (1,2) ≤ black (2,2) componentwise. Output No, which matches.
- **Sample 4:** White (1789,211) ≤ black (1814,712) componentwise. Output No, which matches.

